const articleSchema={'@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'Article',headline:'ATP & WTA Tournaments in Malaysia: History, Venues & Champions',description,inLanguage:'en-MY',datePublished:'2026-06-19',dateModified:'2026-11-05',mainEntityOfPage:new URL(path,SITE.url).href,author:{'@type':'Organization',name:`${SITE.name} Editorial Team`},publisher:{'@type':'Organization',name:SITE.name,logo:{'@type':'ImageObject',url:new URL('/logo.png',SITE.url).href}}};
const crumbs=[{name:'Home',href:'/'},{name:'Tennis',href:'/malaysia-tennis-events/'},{name:'ATP & WTA Malaysia History',href:path}];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path={path} type="article" schema={[articleSchema]} updated={updated}>
  <div class="page-band"><div class="container"><Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} /><h1>ATP &amp; WTA Tournaments in Malaysia</h1><p class="intro">Which professional tour events Malaysia has hosted, when and where they ran, who won, and where to check the records.</p></div></div>
  <section class="section"><div class="container prose">
    <p class="lead">Kuala Lumpur has twice been a regular stop on the men’s and women’s tours: briefly in the early 1990s, and then from 2009 to 2017, when the city hosted an ATP 250 in the autumn and a WTA International event early in the year. Since 2017 there has been no ATP or WTA tour-level event in Malaysia; professional tennis here now runs at ITF World Tennis Tour and junior level.</p>

    <h2 id="timeline">Timeline at a glance</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap" style="min-width:0"><table class="hist">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Years</th><th scope="col">Event</th><th scope="col">Tour &amp; level</th><th scope="col">Where</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1921–1978</td><td>Malayan Championships, then Malaysian Open Championships</td><td>National / open championships (pre-tour)</td><td>Rotated: KL, Penang, Ipoh, Petaling Jaya, Singapore</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1989–1993</td><td>Kuala Lumpur Challenger</td><td>Men · ATP Challenger</td><td>Kuala Lumpur, hard court</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1992</td><td>Malaysian Tennis Classic</td><td>Women · WTA Tier IV</td><td>Kuala Lumpur, indoor hard</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1993–1995</td><td>Kuala Lumpur (Jan 1993), then Kuala Lumpur Open / Salem Open</td><td>Men · ATP World Series</td><td>Kuala Lumpur; hard (1993), indoor carpet (1994–95)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2009–2015</td><td>Proton Malaysian Open (2009–13), Malaysian Open, Kuala Lumpur (2014–15)</td><td>Men · ATP World Tour 250</td><td>Putra Indoor Stadium, Bukit Jalil (indoor hard)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2010–2017</td><td>Malaysian Open (2010), BMW Malaysian Open (2011–16), ALYA WTA Malaysian Open (2017)</td><td>Women · WTA International</td><td>Kuala Lumpur, outdoor hard; venues below</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2025–present</td><td>ITF World Tennis Tour M15 Kuala Lumpur and ITF junior events</td><td>Men’s ITF pro circuit; ITF juniors</td><td>National Tennis Centre, KL, and venues nationwide</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p class="src">Sources: <a href={WIKI_MO} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Malaysian Open (tennis), Wikipedia</a> (secondary) for the pre-2009 events; ATP and WTA archives linked in each table below.</p>

    <h2 id="atp">Men: the ATP Malaysian Open, 2009–2015</h2>
    <p>The ATP 250 event was played indoors on hard courts at the Putra Indoor Stadium in the Bukit Jalil Sports Complex (renamed Axiata Arena in 2017), in late September or early October, at the start of the tour’s Asian swing. After 2015 its place on the ATP calendar went to the new Chengdu Open in China.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap" style="min-width:0"><table class="hist">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Year</th><th scope="col">Tournament</th><th scope="col">Champion</th><th scope="col">Runner-up</th><th scope="col">Final</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{atpModern.map((e)=>(<tr><td><a href={atp(e.y)} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{e.y}</a></td><td>{e.name}</td><td><strong>{e.w}</strong></td><td>{e.r}</td><td class="sc">{e.s}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p class="src">Year links open the official ATP results archive for each edition. Venue and 2014 dates: <a href="https://www.tourism.gov.my/news/trade/view/superb-line-up-for-doubles-event-at-malaysian-open-kuala-lumpur" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tourism Malaysia</a>. Finals as recorded in the ATP archive and on <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2015_Malaysian_Open,_Kuala_Lumpur" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia’s edition pages</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="wta">Women: the WTA Malaysian Open, 2010–2017</h2>
    <p>Kuala Lumpur’s WTA International event was an outdoor hard-court tournament usually held in late February or early March (April in 2014). It moved between three venues in Kuala Lumpur. It was not played at Putra Stadium, which was the men’s venue.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap" style="min-width:0"><table class="hist">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Year</th><th scope="col">Venue</th><th scope="col">Champion</th><th scope="col">Runner-up</th><th scope="col">Final</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{wtaModern.map((e)=>(<tr><td>{e.nodraw ? e.y : <a href={wta(e.y)} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{e.y}</a>}{e.d && <span class="d"> · {e.d}</span>}</td><td>{e.venue}</td><td><strong>{e.w}</strong></td><td>{e.r}</td><td class="sc">{e.s}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p class="src">Year links open the official WTA main-draw sheet for each edition, which gives dates, prize money and surface. No draw sheet was available for 2015. Venues 2010–16: <a href={WIKI_MO} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a> (secondary); 2017 venue: <a href="https://www.malaymail.com/news/sports/2016/12/15/wta-malaysia-returns-to-kl-for-eighth-consecutive-year/1272991" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Malay Mail, 15 Dec 2016</a>.</p>
    <p><strong>Why it ended.</strong> In November 2017 the promoter, Carbon Worldwide, said it would not continue, citing WTA calendar changes that left the February slot poorly placed and difficulty finding sponsors. From late 2013 it had been running the event on a licence leased from the Palermo tournament, which returned to the calendar in 2019 (<a href="https://www.nst.com.my/amp/sports/tennis/2017/11/300014/carbon-worldwide-pulls-plug-wta-malaysian-open" target="_blank" rel="noopener">New Straits Times, 6 Nov 2017</a>).</p>

    <h2 id="1990s">The 1990s tour events</h2>
    <p>Kuala Lumpur first joined the men’s tour in January 1993 and then held an autumn ATP World Series event, sponsored as the Salem Open, from September 1993 to 1995. On the women’s side, the Malaysian Tennis Classic was a WTA Tier IV event in April 1992. Sources disagree on whether a 1993 edition was played, so we list only 1992.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap" style="min-width:0"><table class="hist">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Year</th><th scope="col">Event</th><th scope="col">Champion</th><th scope="col">Runner-up</th><th scope="col">Final</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {atp90s.map((e)=>(<tr><td><a href={atp(e.yr)} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{e.y}</a></td><td>{e.name} · ATP</td><td><strong>{e.w}</strong></td><td>{e.r}</td><td class="sc">{e.s}</td></tr>))}
        <tr><td>1992 (April)</td><td>Malaysian Tennis Classic · WTA</td><td><strong>Yayuk Basuki</strong></td><td>Andrea Strnadová</td><td class="sc">6–3, 6–0</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p class="src">Sources: <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1993_ATP_Tour" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1993 ATP Tour</a> and <a href={WIKI_MO} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Malaysian Open (tennis)</a> on Wikipedia (secondary); year links open the ATP archive. The Kuala Lumpur Challenger (1989–93) is listed in the timeline only: its champions are recorded in secondary sources we have not been able to check against the ATP archive.</p>

    <h2 id="today">Professional tennis in Malaysia today</h2>
    <p>Malaysia now hosts tennis below tour level:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>ITF World Tennis Tour (men):</strong> M15 events at the National Tennis Centre, Kuala Lumpur, which award ATP ranking points.</li>
      <li><strong>ITF junior events:</strong> the J300 Premier Sarawak Cup in Kuching (44th edition in 2026), the J200 and J100 Kuala Lumpur, and J60/J30 events in Seremban, Ipoh, Penang and Petaling Jaya.</li>
      <li><strong>National team ties:</strong> Davis Cup and Billie Jean King Cup zone events at the National Tennis Centre, including both Asia/Oceania Group III (men) and Group II (women) in 2026.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>See the <a href="/tournaments/">tournament calendar</a> for dates and results.</p>

    <h2 id="tours">The tours in brief</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>ATP Tour</strong> (men): Grand Slams (run by the four Slams with the ITF), ATP Masters 1000, 500 and 250. The <strong>ATP Challenger Tour</strong> is a separate second tier.</li>
      <li><strong>WTA Tour</strong> (women): WTA 1000, 500 and 250, with WTA 125 as a second tier.</li>
      <li><strong>ITF World Tennis Tour</strong>: the entry-level professional circuit (M15/M25 for men, W15–W100 for women), which awards ATP and WTA points. It is run by the ITF, not by the ATP or WTA, and also includes the junior tour.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>For how points and rankings work, see the <a href="/tennis-ranking-guide/">tennis ranking guide</a>.</p>

    <Faq items={faqs} />
    <p class="callout">{SITE.name} is an independent website and is not affiliated with the ATP, WTA, ITF, TennisMalaysia or any tournament named on this page. Event names are used to identify them. Tournament records are taken from the official archives and sources linked above; if you spot an error, please <a href="/contact/">tell us</a>.</p>
    <div class="related callout info"><strong>Related:</strong> <a href="/tennis-ranking-guide/">Tennis ranking guide</a> · <a href="/malaysian-tennis-players/">Malaysian tennis players</a> · <a href="/tournaments/">2026 calendar &amp; results</a></div>
  </div></section>
  <style>
    .hist{min-width:0;font-size:14.5px}
    .hist td{vertical-align:top}
    .hist .sc{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:13px;white-space:nowrap}
    .hist .d{color:var(--slate);font-size:12.5px}
    .src{font-size:13.5px;color:var(--slate)}
    @media (max-width:640px){ .hist{min-width:560px} }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
